sidgwick-methods-3.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1.2 have "¬designed_for_discovery" and "¬designed_for_discovery → ¬secondary_evil_intended"; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the crime was designed for discovery" is true, which the second allows
sidgwick-methods-3.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1 and sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the crime was designed for discovery" is true and "the secondary evil caused by the crime was intended by the criminal" is false. Is S5, "But it remains true that it was not designed for discovery; and therefore that this part of the evil caused by the crime was not intended by the criminal.", true then?
sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1 has "no_harm_intended_many ∨ ¬secondary_evil_intended ∧ ¬secrecy_lessens_gravity → ¬gravity_tracks_intended_harm" and "easy_principles → gravity_tracks_intended_harm ∨ utilitarian_reading ∧ ¬legal_fiction"; sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1.2 and sidgwick-methods-3.logic.r1.3 have "intends_right_sometimes ∨ no_harm_intended_many → ¬gravity_tracks_intended_harm" and "secondary_evil_great_part ∧ secrecy_cuts_off_secondary ∧ ¬secrecy_lessens_gravity → ¬gravity_tracks_intended_harm" and "¬gravity_tracks_intended_harm → ¬easy_principles"; they differ when only "it is easy to state clear principles for determining the gravity of crimes by the intention only", "sometimes, as with the patriotic rebel, the intention of the criminal is to do what is right and good", "in many cases the criminal does not intend to cause any actual harm to any sentient being", "a crime is rendered less grave by being kept perfectly secret", "a great part of the harm done by a crime is the secondary evil of the alarm and insecurity which it causes", "the secondary evil is cut off by complete secrecy", "the secondary evil caused by the crime was intended by the criminal" and "we say heinousness depends on the loss of happiness such acts would generally cause if unpunished, and suppose the criminal aware of this" are true, which the first allows
